{{!-- The document, and the parts of the page that are the same everywhere.

The markup is Quire Ink's, element for element and class for class, because the stylesheet
this theme ships is Quire Ink's and it binds to those names. Where Ghost wants a helper the
helper goes in; where it wants a different shape, the shape here wins — a `<div class="wrap">`
is not negotiable with a sheet that lays the page out from it. --}}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{@site.locale}}"
    data-palette="{{@custom.palette}}"
    {{#if @custom.terminal_chrome}}data-ide-chrome="on"{{/if}}>
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>{{meta_title}}</title>

    {{!-- THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is the blog engine's own: the static sheet, then
    the generated half, which is generated precisely because it has to win. `.rail` is a
    slide-out drawer in the base sheet and only the computed media query in the tokens sheet
    promotes it into the desktop gutter. Enqueued the intuitive way round — variables first,
    because everything reads them — the drawer rule wins on source order and the table of
    contents silently never appears on any desktop screen. --}}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{asset "css/quireink-base.css"}}">
    {{#if @custom.terminal_chrome}}<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{asset "css/quireink-ide.css"}}">{{/if}}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{asset "css/quireink-tokens.css"}}">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{asset "css/quireink-alias.css"}}">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{asset "css/bridge.css"}}">

    {{!-- BLOCKING, and in the head, which is the one script in this theme that is. It
    installs the shim that answers `/api/*` for the two copied bundles, and it defines the
    two functions the inline calls further down the page make WHILE the parser is still
    there — the contents rail and the timeline are built before first paint, so neither
    arrives as a jump. 3 KB from the same origin, before anything is painted anyway. --}}
    <script src="{{asset "js/ghost-bridge.js"}}"></script>

    {{!-- LAST in the head, so the owner's own code injection wins. Ghost's card CSS also
    lands here and is NOT a problem: every rule in bridge.css is scoped under `.prose`, so it
    outranks an unscoped `.kg-*` rule on specificity and does not need to come after it. --}}
    {{ghost_head}}
</head>

<body class="{{body_class}}"
    data-default-scheme="{{@custom.default_scheme}}"
    {{~> "generated-labels"}}
    {{~#is "post, page"}}{{> "generated-labels-post"}}{{/is}}>

{{#is "post"}}{{!-- Markup plus a scroll-driven CSS animation, with no script behind it: it
works with JavaScript off and costs nothing on the main thread. `@supports` in the sheet
hides it on an engine without scroll timelines, so the failure mode is absence. --}}
<div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"><div class="progress-fill"></div></div>
{{/is}}

<div class="wrap{{#if @custom.book_typography}} book-text{{/if}}">
{{> "site-header"}}

{{{body}}}

{{> "site-footer"}}
</div>

{{!-- Quire Ink's own reader bundles, copied byte for byte by tools/extract.ts. `core` is
the chrome — palette switch, rail drawer, search overlay, sign-up, grid view; `post` is the
article — contents scrollspy, book mode, lightbox, code copy. `post.js` is only loaded where
there is an article for it to work on. --}}
<script defer src="{{asset "js/core.js"}}"></script>
{{#is "post, page"}}<script defer src="{{asset "js/post.js"}}"></script>{{/is}}

{{ghost_foot}}
</body>
</html>
